social media is playing a major role in  the rescue efforts Twitter and Facebook  pages are filled not only with posts  requesting help but offering help as  well people connected to a Galveston  County nursing home say the emergence of  this image online this unforgettable  image helps speed up the arrival of help  Omar Villafranca is in Houston this  morning Omar good morning this may be  the most tweeted about natural disaster  in history if anything because there's  more people on social media now than  there were 12 years ago during Hurricane  Katrina and people are coming out and  they're posting about things in their  neighborhood like water levels like this  one here or asking for help but it's  still not clear whether all the postings  are hurting or helping rescue efforts  this is what it looked like inside the  la vita bella' nursing home in dickinson  texas about 30 miles southeast of  downtown Houston Ruth Miller was one of  the 15 elderly residents stranded suzhen  Beaubourg is her sister it already  called 911 the TV was saying don't call  9-1-1 anymore and nobody is coming to  rescue them the woman who owns the  nursing home took this photo her  son-in-law posted it on Twitter with the  caption need help ASAP emergency  services please retweet it was retweeted  hundreds of times we spoke with him on  the phone from his Florida home I don't  have a large Twitter following I do a  whole lot of tweeting but I was like  well let's put it on Twitter and see if  we can get some action from it about  three hours after they tweeted the photo  all 15 senior citizens were rescued our  cameras were rolling as three women we  believe were part of that group were  lifted onto a rescue vehicle my thanks  are definitely out too not just the  authorities but also to all the Twitter  followers and actually believe the story  and retweeted it and got everything in  motion an official we spoke with says  rescuers were already on the way when  the photo started getting attention  online it was just one of many social  media posts calling attention to people  needing rescues including a Facebook  group with over 80,000 followers formed  during the storm  but agencies including  FEMA the US Coast Guard and the Houston  Police Department asked people not to  use social media for rescue requests  Houston Police said 900 is the best way  to capture your request and make sure it  is properly dispatched social media has  been helpful in one way Galveston County  put out a call for help and a Cajun Navy  that you've been hearing about while  they are in the Houston area and they  are already out helping to rescue people  Omar thank you
 

